<*>

\begin{center}
{\Large Structure and Compilation of C-Programs}
\end{center}

<*>
<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
\section{Types}
On the most basic level, the type of a variable identifies how many
bits it has, and how we interpret its bits.   A classic example is
the difference between the types {\tt uint8\_t} and {\tt char}.  Both
of these types have lengths of 8 bits (1 byte).
If our 8 bits in memory contain
for example

\vspace{0.1in}\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|}
\hline
0&1&1&0&0&0&0&1\\\hline
\end{tabular}\end{center}
\vspace{0.1in}

then if the 8-bit block is declared {\tt uint8\_t} they will mean 97
but if the 8-bit block  is declared {\tt char}
they will mean `a'.  In computer memory {\it everything} is just
an ordered set of ones and zeros.  The type determines how we
work with these ones and zeros.  For example we can multiply two
integers,
but if we apply the character meaning (i.e. the {\tt char} type),
then we can not meaningfully multiply them.
Similar examples could be given for floats, ints,
etc.

<*chn>
\paragraph{``Types of types''} In non-embedded applications,
we don't worry as much about the bits.  The mainstream types
such as {\tt int, float} etc., may have different numbers of
bits in different C-compilers or different CPU instruction sets.
In many cases you don't want to or need to worry about whether
an {\tt int} is say 32 bits or 64 bits so you just declare your
variable an {\tt int}.  This way your code can be compiled to
different hardware and still work ({\it if} you don't need
 the second 32 bits.)

In embedded systems that often won't do.  For example, you
may have to write code to control a timer inside your chip.
If its control register is 16 bits wide, you need to be able
to work with 16-bit integers.  This is why the more specific
types like {\tt unint16\_t} exist.  Generally if the number
of bits isn't right there in the type name its not a good
idea to assume you know how many bits are in that type.
<*>

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
\section{Creating a variable or function in C}

<*hcn>
Unlike Python, C requires a lot of information from you before you can
create a variable or function (i.e.
allocate memory to data or code).
Variables and functions have a lot
of properties that the programmer must {\it declare}.
In C programs this is a two-step process:  first we ``declare'' a
variable or function, and then we ``define'' it.

Declarations
tell the compiler how a variable or function must be used but do not
actually create or allocate memory for them.

Definitions
put a variable or function in a specific place, and the definition
must look almost identical to the declaration, but may also initialize
the variable.  In the case of function definitions, of course
the definition includes the code describing what the function will
do.

There's a lot of repetition or similarity between declarations
and definitions, so we'll define them carefully since you need to
use both.

<*chn>
\subsection{Variable declarations}

\begin{itemize}
  \item Simplest:   {\tt type name;}  \\
  {\tt type} defines the variable as {\tt float, int} etc.  In
  embedded programming we use a lot of specialized types such as
  {\tt unint16\_t}  which means ``unsigned 16-bit integer type''.
  \item Advanced:  {\tt [extern][volatile] type name [= value];}\\
  in this form we have qualifiers (brackets, `[]' mean an optional
  qualifier, don't type them in your code):
  \begin{itemize}
    \item {\tt[extern]:} the definition of this variable will be
    found in a different file in the same build.
    \item {\tt [volatile]:} this is a special qualifier for real-time
    and embedded systems.   It tells the compiler this variable could
    change value without warning in the middle of code execution.  This
    can happen when code is interrupted by an interrupt.   Compilers try
    to optimize performance with various tricks that would break without
    {\tt volatile}.  It's rare, but we definitely need it for interrupt
    driven coding.
    \item For example:\\
    {\tt extern int x;}\\
    means an integer named {\tt x}
    which is not defined in this file (extern) but will be defined
    in another .c file.
  \end{itemize}
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Function declarations (also known as ``Function Prototypes'')}
\begin{itemize}
  \item Simplest:   {\tt type name(arg1type, arg2type,...);}  \\
  for example:\\
  {\tt float hypotenuse(float a, float b);}\\
  note we don't code the function in the declaration, we are just
  telling the compiler what it needs to know when it comes up in
  .c files.  It is ok to have no arguments.
  \item Advanced: {\tt [static][extern] type name(arg1type, arg2type,...);}  \\
  {\tt static} means that this function will only be used within
  the current file. {\tt extern} has the same meaning for function
  declarations as for variable.
\end{itemize}



\subsection{Variable Definitions}
\begin{itemize}
  \item Simplest: {\tt type name;}  \\
  this sets up the variable in a .c file but does not initialize
  it to any value.  Fun fact, this may put random bits into your
  variable at the start of some code.
  \item Safer: {\tt type name= 0;}\\
  In the definition you can assign its initial value which may
  be safer for the logic of your program.   This form is equivalent
  to\\
  {\tt type name;}\\
  {\tt name = 0;}\\
  Only the second line is actual running code the first line only
  allocates memory and defines the location for the object in memory.
\end{itemize}


\subsection{Function definitions}
\begin{itemize}
  \item Simplest:  {\tt type name(arg1type arg1name, arg2type arg2name,...); \{function code here\}} \\
  Note that here we have put names on the argument list after
  each type.
  \item Advanced: {\tt [static] type name(arg1type arg1name, arg2type arg2name,...); \{function code here\} }\\
  {\tt static} has the same definition as in function declarations.
\end{itemize}




<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
%%%%** Section 5
\section{Variable Scope}

\begin{itemize}
	\item Variable declared  inside a function is {\it local}.
	\item Variable declared outside a function is visible anywhere
inside that ``.c" file.
	\item Variable declared outside a function can also be visible in
other ``.c" files --- if {\tt extern} is used.
	\item Variables declared with prefix {\tt extern} must be declared
outside a function in {\it another } ``.c" file.
\end{itemize}

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}


%%%%** Section 6
\section{Function Scope}
\begin{itemize}

  \item
  Functions cannot be used without a function prototype.

  \item
  Each ``.c" file must have a function prototype for each function which is used
  in that ``.c" file.

  \item
  {\tt \#include } can help manage your function prototypes.

\end{itemize}

<s>\end{slide}

<s>\begin{slide}

%%%%** Section 1 
\section{C Programs in Multiple Files}

A C program is typically written in multiple files.

Simpler editing (?)

Helps modularity and multiple authors.

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
    
File: \framebox{main.c}
\lstset{language=c}
\lstset{basicstyle=\ttfamily, commentstyle=\textit}
\begin{lstlisting}[frame=trbl]{}
main()
  {
   ... code  ...
  }

fcn1()
  {
  ... code ...
  }
\end{lstlisting}


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
File: \framebox{functions.c}

\lstset{language=c}
\lstset{basicstyle=\ttfamily, commentstyle=\textit}
\begin{lstlisting}[frame=trbl]{}
fnc2()
  {
   ... code  ...
  }

fcn3()
  {
  ... code ...
  }

... etc ...
\end{lstlisting}


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

Execution starts with first line of {\tt main() }.

All functions (except {\tt main()}) require a {\bf function prototype}
in any file in which they will be called or defined. 

All functions are visible from any file (but prototype must be supplied in the file).

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
%%%%** Section 2 
\section{Include Files (.h files)}
Text can be read in to your C-Source file just prior to compilation. 

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
File: \framebox{A.c}

\lstset{language=c}
\lstset{basicstyle=\ttfamily, commentstyle=\textit}
\begin{lstlisting}[frame=trbl]{}
#include X.h
main()
  {
   ... code  ...
  }
 
\end{lstlisting}
File: \framebox{X.h}

\lstset{language=c}
\lstset{basicstyle=\ttfamily, commentstyle=\textit}
\begin{lstlisting}[frame=trbl]{}
/*
 hello, this is just a comment
*/
\end{lstlisting}

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
output:

\lstset{language=c}
\lstset{basicstyle=\ttfamily, commentstyle=\textit}
\begin{lstlisting}[frame=trbl]{}
/*
 hello, this is just a comment
*/
main()
  {
   ... code  ...
  }
 
\end{lstlisting}


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

Reasons:
\begin{itemize}
	\item {\tt \#include} text which should be {\bf the same} in multiple ``.c" files. 
	\item keep and edit just 1 copy.
	\item less typing (as in fewer keystrokes to hit)
    \item ensures consistency.
\end{itemize}

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

    \includegraphics[width=4.5in]{cprog_figs/includes_multi_protos.png}
    
    A single {\tt .h} file introduces the exact same function prototypes
    and data declarations into multiple files without having to maintain 
    separate identical copies in multiple files. 
    
<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

<*chn>
\subsection{What goes in {\tt xxxxx.h}}

\paragraph{Include files ({\tt xxxxx.h})}
\begin{itemize}
  \item Variable declarations.\\
  These inform the compiler how to recognize the type and optional qualifiers
  it may encounters in
  {\tt .c} files. Never initialize a variable in the {\tt .h} file.
  \item Function declarations (aka Function Prototypes)\\
  These inform the compiler how to recognize function details it encounters in
  {\tt .c} files.
\end{itemize}
\paragraph{Code files ({\tt yyyy.c})}
\begin{itemize}
  \item Variable definitions.\\
  Actually allocate memory and optionally initialize the variable.
  Initialization at this time is best practice.
  \item Function definitions.\\
  Define the code for the function (which will also allocate memory for that code).
\end{itemize}

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}


%%%%** Section 3 
\section{Other C pre-processor functions}

%%%%** Section 3.1
\subsection{{\tt\#define }}

like the search-replace function in your word processor.

<!s>ex:

{\tt \#define  TRUE  1}

c pre-processor will replace all occurrences of {\tt TRUE} with {\tt 1}.


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

%%%%** Section 3.2
\subsection{\tt\#ifdef / \#endif}

C pre-processor can turn on-off  blocks of code before compilation.

<!s>ex:

\lstset{language=c}
\lstset{basicstyle=\ttfamily, commentstyle=\textit}
\begin{lstlisting}[]{}
#define LINUX 1
//#define WINDOWS 1

...

#ifdef WINDOWS
     ... windows-specific code ...
#endif 

#ifdef LINUX
     ... linux-specific code ...
#endif 
\end{lstlisting}


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

%%%%** Section 3.3
\subsection{C pre-processor overview}

<s>\includegraphics[width=4.0in]{cprog_figs/c-building.png}
<chn>\includegraphics[width=5.5in]{cprog_figs/c-building.png}
    
<*>

The C pre-processor applies some convenience features to your code before 
compilation.  Specifically, the {\tt \#ifdef} macro can turn on and off C-code
until {\tt \#endif} is encountered.   You might use this to define a constant only
if it has not already been defined by some earlier statements in a {\tt \#include}'d .h file.  If {\tt \#includes} were processed before the {\tt \#ifdef}, their statements
are taken into account when evaluating {\tt \#ifdef}.
Code from files indicated in {\tt \#include} statements (typically {\tt .h } files) is merged into your code.  Finally, all {\tt \#defines} are evaluated and the 
substitutions are made in your code.   Most likely, you will not need to worry about
these complexities unless you are working on very big packages of code.   For 
ECE474, you {\bf  probably} do not need {\tt \#ifdef}s at all. 
<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

<*>
%%%%** Section 4 
\section{Building a C program}

<*hnc>
Converting a package of several ``.c'' and ``.h'' files can be a complex
process which is fortunately automated by a build system.

{\bf Compilation} is the process of translating your C code into machine language
instructions.   Compilation produces a binary file of machine language instructions, called the {\it Object} file, but it is incomplete in one key way.
Addresses such as the location of a variable or the
start point of a loop are computed {\it relative} to the start of the
code file.  The next step is {\it linking} which puts all the object
files into one sequential binary code block by converting all
the relative memory references to be relative to the first code block.
Now we have a single {\it executable} binary file which can run. The linker
will also include code from libraries, such as a device driver, which
somebody else wrote some other time.  It will be stitched in with your
code so that your code can use it.

<*>

\begin{enumerate}
	\item Run C pre-processor on the ``.c" file
	\item Compile output into ``object" file.
	\begin{itemize}
		\item Incomplete machine language program
		\item ``.o" file for each ``.c" file
	\end{itemize}
\end{enumerate}

{\bf Then: } ``Link" all ``.o" files, and library files,
	to create ``executable" binary file. 
<s>\end{slide}
